{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And you, be fruitful and multiply</b> — Its plain sense is like its midrashic sense, that it is a commandment. For what is said regarding Adam (Genesis 1:28) and regarding the sons of Noah (ibid. 9:1) together with \"and Elohim blessed them\" is a blessing, as in the case of what is said regarding the fish (ibid. 1:22). And because He spoke concerning the rest of the living creatures and said, \"and they shall swarm on the earth and be fruitful and multiply on the earth\" (ibid. 8:17), He said here, 'And you' — the human being — 'be fruitful and multiply.' And He said, \"swarm on the earth and multiply in it,\" because He repeats the commandment for emphasis, to say that they should engage in it with all their ability, or that He commands them to settle all the earth, as I explained in the portion of Bereshit (1:20). And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 9:7) that according to its first occurrence it is a blessing, and here it is a commandment, and according to its midrashic sense, it is to draw an association between one who does not engage in procreation and a shedder of blood. But they derived this midrash only from the juxtaposition, whereas the verse was written as a commandment, and the first occurrence as a blessing. And so they said: \"Now is not procreation that was said to the sons of Noah a commandment? For it is written, 'And you, be fruitful and multiply,'\" etc., as is found in Sanhedrin (59b)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The word שרצו in the phrase \"swarm on the earth\" (Genesis 9:7) appears in the biblical text; Ramban is explaining the repetition of the root שרץ from Genesis 8:17 in the command to humanity."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:26:28.108865748Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}